WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.529
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.529 --> 00:00:09.910
With today's graphics technology, we can enjoy
many really smooth videos that were created

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:11.620
using 60 frames per second.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:17.520
We love it too, and we hope that you noticed
that our last hundred or maybe even more episodes

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.610
have been available in 60hz.

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:25.060
However, it oftentimes happens that we're
given videos that have anything from 20 to

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:26.820
30 frames per second.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:32.090
This means that if we play them on a 60 fps
timeline, half or even more of these frames

00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:33.969
will not provide any new information.

00:00:33.969 --> 00:00:39.559
As we try to slow down the videos for some
nice slow-motion action, this ratio is even

00:00:39.559 --> 00:00:42.910
worse, creating an extremely choppy output
video.

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:47.379
Fortunately, there are techniques that are
able to guess what happens in these intermediate

00:00:47.379 --> 00:00:48.920
frames and give them to us.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:51.859
This is what we call frame interpolation.

00:00:51.859 --> 00:00:56.809
We have had some previous experiments in this
area where we tried to create an amazing slow

00:00:56.809 --> 00:00:59.749
motion version of a video with some bubbles
merging.

00:00:59.749 --> 00:01:05.570
A simple and standard way of doing frame interpolation
is called frame blending, which is a simple

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:08.520
averaging of the closest two known frames.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:13.620
The more advanced techniques are optical flow-based,
which is a method to determine what motions

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:18.710
happened between the two frames, and create
new images based on that knowledge, leading

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:21.570
to higher quality results in most cases.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:26.390
This technique uses a convolutional neural
network to accomplish something similar, but

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:30.690
in the end, it doesn't give us an image, but
a set of convolution kernels.

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:35.690
This is a transformation that is applied to
the previous and the next frame to produce

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:36.690
the intermediate image.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:41.610
It is not the image itself, but a recipe of
how to produce it, if you will.

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:47.180
We've had a ton of fun with convolutions earlier,
where we used them to create beautiful subsurface

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:52.660
scattering effects for translucent materials
in real time, and our more loyal Fellow Scholars

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:58.240
remember that at some point, I also pulled
out my guitar and showed what it would sound

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.650
like inside a church using a convolution-based
reverberation technique.

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:06.380
The links are available in the video description,
make sure to check them out!

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:10.810
Since we have a neural network over here,
it goes without saying that the training takes

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:16.930
place on a large number of before-after image
pairs, so that the network is able to produce

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:18.250
these convolution kernels.

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:22.850
Of course, to validate this algorithm, we
also need to have access to a ground truth

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:27.930
reference to compare against - we can accomplish
this by withholding some information about

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:33.769
a few intermediate frames so we have the true
images which the algorithm would have to reproduce

00:02:33.769 --> 00:02:34.819
without seeing it.

00:02:34.819 --> 00:02:38.750
Kinda like giving a test to a student when
we already know the answers.

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:41.150
You can see such a comparison here.

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:52.579
And now, let's have a look at these results!

00:02:52.579 --> 00:02:57.599
As you can see, they are extremely smooth,
and the technique retains a lot of high-frequency

00:02:57.599 --> 00:02:59.270
details in these images.

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:04.290
The videos also seem temporally coherent,
which means that it's devoid of the annoying

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:08.900
flickering effect where the reconstruction
takes place in a way that's a bit different

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:10.529
in each subsequent frame.

00:03:10.529 --> 00:03:14.459
None of that happens here, which is an excellent
property of this technique.

00:03:14.459 --> 00:03:18.840
The python source code for this technique
is available and is free for non-commercial

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:19.840
uses.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:23.260
I've put a link in the description, if you
have given it a try and have some results

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:27.849
of your own, make sure to post them in the
comments section or our subreddit discussion.

00:03:27.849 --> 00:03:29.689
The link is available in the description.

00:03:29.689 --> 00:03:33.760
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

